<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">John II of Portugal</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1455">1455</a>–<a href="/years/1495">1495</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as João II · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-aviz">House of Aviz</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">John II of Portugal was a king of the late fifteenth century who vigorously promoted exploration along the African coast. During his reign <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bartolomeu-dias">Bartolomeu Dias</a> rounded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cape-of-good-hope">Cape of Good Hope</a>, and he negotiated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-tordesillas">Treaty of Tordesillas</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>. His policies were central to the foundation of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/john-ii-of-portugal.jpg" alt="John II of Portugal" width="309" height="420" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:IOANNES_QVARTVS_PORTVGALIAE_REX_(Kunsthistorisches_Museum).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its dealings with Portuguese traders in the fifteenth century came at a turning point in West African commerce and contributed to the gradual shift from trans-Saharan to Atlantic-oriented trade; in 1488 the exiled Jolof prince Bemoim was received and baptised at the court of <b>João II</b> in Lisbon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/jolof-empire">Jolof Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Under <b>João II</b>, Bartolomeu Dias rounded the Cape of Good Hope in 1488 and showed that an oceanic route to Asia was possible.</blockquote><a href="/empire/portuguese-empire">Portuguese Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Portuguese priests baptised the ManiKongo, who took the name João in honour of Portugal's King <b>João II</b>, and his principal nobles were baptised too.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/may-3">3 May in history</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1482, on the orders of <b>King John II</b>, Diogo de Azambuja built the fortress of Sao Jorge da Mina at Elmina, after negotiating with a local chief remembered as Caramansa.</blockquote><a href="/state/portuguese-gold-coast">Portuguese Gold Coast</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Castle of Saint George, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. Died in Alvor, in what is now <a href="/countries/portugal">Portugal</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.13&amp;year=1481" aria-label="Castle of Saint George on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4323 774 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of John II of Portugal"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4773 1044h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4773 1044h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.71,-9.13&amp;year=1481">What was here in 1481? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
